1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,354
Every event contract gives you a price. It does not tell you who could know first.

2
00:00:05,554 --> 00:00:14,484
LevelField is a pre-trade risk layer for DreamDEX. It measures structural information asymmetry before a trader or agent takes a side.

3
00:00:14,684 --> 00:00:22,776
Start with a captured DreamDEX price binary on Somnia Shannon. LevelField scores it three out of one hundred: low risk.

4
00:00:22,976 --> 00:00:31,140
No participant controls the global reference price. Its outcome is public, and disclosure is nearly simultaneous.

5
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:38,145
Now change only the event. This curated reference contract resolves on one person's private decision.

6
00:00:38,345 --> 00:00:42,639
The same engine returns ninety-five out of one hundred: high risk.

7
00:00:42,839 --> 00:00:51,723
One person controls the outcome. No clear restriction stops an early knower from trading. Circuit breaker one sets a high-risk floor.

8
00:00:51,923 --> 00:00:59,802
That three-to-ninety-five contrast is the product. LevelField explains who can know first, not which side will win.

9
00:01:00,002 --> 00:01:03,917
It does not allege wrongdoing or detect live insider activity.

10
00:01:04,117 --> 00:01:12,095
Under the interface is a public, five-dimension anchor library. A model classifies each dimension against those anchors.

11
00:01:12,295 --> 00:01:20,065
Every evidence quote must match the contract's exact words. Code rejects instruction-like text aimed at the assessor.

12
00:01:20,265 --> 00:01:26,954
If information is missing, LevelField defaults conservatively to level four instead of guessing low.

13
00:01:27,154 --> 00:01:36,510
Then deterministic code applies fixed weights, cross-dimension rules, and circuit breakers. The model never generates the numeric score.

14
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:43,646
Here, an agent calls the real LevelField MCP server over standard input and output before it acts.

15
00:01:43,846 --> 00:01:50,125
The policy is visible: low or moderate risk can proceed. Elevated or high risk is declined.

16
00:01:50,325 --> 00:01:59,275
The DreamDEX example returns PROCEED at three. The individual-decision case returns DECLINE at ninety-five, with the reason attached.

17
00:01:59,475 --> 00:02:11,441
After a provenance-complete republish, each current score will bind its band, five dimensions, method hash, timestamp, and immutable source on Somnia Shannon.

18
00:02:11,641 --> 00:02:16,978
The verifier reads every field back and fails closed on anything missing or changed.

19
00:02:17,178 --> 00:02:27,966
Across sixteen curated contracts, scores span three to ninety-five. Category risk rises in the expected order, with a Spearman rho of point nine three.

20
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:40,762
Sixty-nine software tests and eight smart-contract tests pass. The official DreamDEX SDK independently cross-checks active-market discovery, read-only and without a private key.

21
00:02:40,962 --> 00:02:53,363
We delivered eleven evidence-backed SDK and documentation findings. As DreamDEX grows, LevelField helps agents, venues, and traders know who can know before they do.
